title: Agent-side conflict resolution
status: draft
created: 2026-11-23
author: Luke Carrier
approach: "Extend the existing reconciliation path rather than add a parallel one. The four-tree plan in `orchestrator/source.rs` already classifies every sandbox state and already reads `Vcs::conflicts` for the shared branch, so resolution becomes a second consumer of the same classification with three explicit actions: begin, finish and abandon. Begin writes the conflicted revision into the sandbox workspace that already exists. Finish asks the backend whether the working copy still conflicts, reports the per-side difference, and then performs the host write itself. Abandon rebuilds the merge from its recorded parents and writes it again. The contract grows three operations and one field, both backends implement them, and no backend gains a path into the sandbox.\n\nNo requirement in the spec is marked `assumption`. Two claims carry the most weight and both come from the map's probes rather than from this plan. Git answers the three sides from `merge-tree` stage entries, which the T-023 output shows as blob ids per stage. jj answers the three sides from the parents of the resolution revision, which T-003 establishes with `jj diff --from <base> --to <ours>`. If either fails during implementation, the per-side report degrades to paths only and FR-007 still holds."
architecture: "Four layers, each with one job. The MCP tools translate arguments and append the workspace block. `orchestrator/resolve.rs` owns the three actions and the host write. The `vcs` contract gains `blob` and richer `Conflict` values while keeping revisions and trees opaque. The backends answer from git2 and from the `jj` binary as they do today.\n\n{{mermaid: architecture.mmd}}\n\nThe plan in `orchestrator/source.rs` does not change. `Action` gains no resolution variants because resolution is not a planning decision: the plan already says whether the shared branch conflicts, and `begin` reads that state rather than recomputing it.\n\nThe workspace directory stays the only tree. Resolution replaces its content, and the snapshot that follows every mutating tool commits to the revision under resolution because `set_sandbox_tip` already points there under `Bound` and `AdoptContent` already rewrites it otherwise."
technologies[4]{name,role}:
  git2,"Reads trees and blobs for the Git backend, and for jj's co-located clone"
  jj,"Shelled out to for `resolve --list`, `new` and `squash`; pinned minimum version as the jj ADR does"
  rmcp,"Carries the new tool arguments and the workspace block"
  serde and schemars,"Derive the MCP argument schemas and the serialized reports"
components[12]{name,purpose,details}:
  vcs conflict type,Richer conflict values,"`vcs::Conflict` gains `hunks`, `sides` and `base`. `sides` counts content sides so jj's two-sided answer and Git's three index stages agree; `base` reports whether a base exists. Every constructor and test in `vcs/mod.rs` and `testing/vcs.rs` follows."
  vcs blob operation,Serves one file from one tree,"New `Vcs::blob(tree, path) -> Option<Vec<u8>>`. It is the primitive `read` needs for a side and it keeps tree identities opaque. Git resolves the path inside a tree with git2; jj resolves it against the clone's Git backend, which `tree_id` already uses."
  Git conflict check,Answers whether a tree conflicts,"`GitVcs::conflicts` scans the tree a snapshot would commit for lines beginning with the three markers, and counts regions per path. It replaces the current `Unsupported` return at `vcs/git/scm.rs:886`. It reads trees, never the index."
  Git conflict sides,"Answers base, ours and theirs","`GitVcs::conflict_sides` reads the stage entries from a `merge-tree` run against the recorded base and returns their blob ids as trees. The T-023 output gives one entry per stage per path."
  jj conflict counts,Keeps the side count,"`vcs::jj::ops::conflicts` parses `path    N-sided conflict` and keeps N instead of taking the first token and dropping the rest. Region counts come from the materialised tree."
  jj conflict sides,"Answers base, ours and theirs","`JjVcs::conflict_sides` derives the three trees from the revision's parents through `jj diff` ranges, which T-003 established. It replaces the current `Unsupported` return at `vcs/jj/mod.rs:669`."
  orchestrator resolve,"Owns begin, finish and abandon","New `orchestrator/resolve.rs`. Begin refuses an open resolution, asks the backend for the conflicted revision, publishes it into a copied sandbox and records the `synced` revision plus the sandbox tip it replaced. The revision under resolution is read fresh from the working copy and never stored, because resolving rewrites it. Finish re-reads the conflict state, builds the per-side report from the recorded tip and the shared branch, advances the branch by compare-and-swap, records the new `synced` and clears the record. Abandon hands the backend the recorded tip and publishes whatever it restores. It names no ref and no backend."
  workspace materialisation,Changes what the sandbox holds,"For Git and jj `Copied` it writes the conflicted revision through `materialise` and uploads it, so ignored paths survive. For jj `Bound` the backend's `jj new` is the materialisation and nothing is uploaded. A backend that cannot create the conflict refuses rather than editing an existing revision."
  MCP sandbox-reconcile,Carries the three actions,"The tool argument gains `action` with the values `begin`, `finish` and `abandon`, and loses host staging. It returns the revision it started from, or the report and the resulting tip on finish, and always appends the workspace block."
  MCP sandbox-status,The conflict report,"New tool. It returns the source state, the revisions, and for each conflicted path the hunk count, the side count and whether a base exists. It returns no region text. The map already names this tool and it does not exist yet."
  MCP read side,One file from one side,"`ReadArgs` gains `side` with the values `base`, `ours` and `theirs`. When it is absent the tool runs `cat` in the sandbox as today. When it is present the tool asks the orchestrator for `conflict_sides` then `blob` and returns the text, so the sandbox still sees one tree."
  Host staging demotion,Keeps the operator path,"`reconcile` with `--finish` and `--abort` in `cli.rs` keeps staging a linked worktree on the host. The MCP tool stops calling it, so no MCP call creates a host worktree. The orchestrator functions stay where they are."
dataFlow: "Begin. The agent calls `sandbox-reconcile` with `begin`. The orchestrator classifies the sandbox, reads the shared and sandbox revisions and asks `Vcs::conflicts` for the shared branch. When the shared branch is clean the call fails and reports the state. Otherwise it snapshots the workspace, materialises the conflicted revision into the workspace directory, records that a resolution is open with the `synced` revision it began from, and returns the workspace block with the conflicted paths and their counts.\n\nReading. The agent calls `sandbox-status` and gets paths with hunk and side counts. It calls `read` with `side: ours` for a path and gets that side's bytes, which lets it run a three-way merge instead of editing markers.\n\nEditing. `write`, `patch` and `bash` behave as they do today. Each one plans, runs the tool and snapshots, so the resolution is committed to the revision under resolution.\n\nFinish. The agent calls `sandbox-reconcile` with `finish`. The orchestrator re-reads the conflict state. If the backend still reports a conflict the call fails and names the paths. Otherwise it builds the per-side report from the recorded sandbox tip, the shared branch and the resolved tree, advances the shared branch to the revision under resolution, records it as `synced` and returns the report. The advance is a compare-and-swap, so a branch that moved is replanned. A jj conflicted tip is the exception: the write squashes the resolution into the commit that introduced the conflict.\n\nAbandon. The agent calls `sandbox-reconcile` with `abandon`. The orchestrator hands the backend the recorded sandbox tip, publishes whatever the backend restores and clears the open resolution. No repository-wide operation runs."
deployment: "No deployment change. The resolution path is local and lazy, the jj binary is already a prerequisite of the jj backend, and the Git marker scan uses the repository the backend already opened."
